Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to build a
Python terminal PDF text pager for Termux, covering purpose, inputs/outputs,
dependencies, logic, error handling, edge cases, and usage.
Only the prompt is output.
Build a single-file Python 3 command-line tool, intended to run under Termux on
Android (shebang pointing to the Termux Python path), that displays the
extracted text of one or more PDF files in a scrollable, paged terminal viewer.
**Purpose** The script accepts one or more PDF file paths, extracts text from
every page, splits that text into terminal-sized screens, and lets the user page
through them interactively with single-keypress navigation.
It should also support a non-interactive mode for dumping or inspecting the
content. **Command-line interface** Use argparse with the description "Display
extracted PDF text in a scrollable terminal pager." Accept: - One or more
positional `paths` arguments naming PDF files. - Options controlling the backend
used for extraction, page range selection, the number of worker processes
(default 8), and any flag that forces non-interactive output to stdout. -
Validate that each path exists and has a `.pdf` suffix (case-insensitive);
report clear errors for missing or non-PDF inputs and exit with a non-zero
status when no valid input remains. - Parse arguments from a passed-in argument
sequence so the entry point can be called with custom argv for testing.
**Dependencies** - Standard library only, apart from `loguru` for logging
(configure a sensible default sink and level; log warnings for skipped pages or
backend fallbacks, errors for failures). - Use `termios` and `tty` for raw
single-character keyboard input on a Unix TTY; restore terminal attributes in a
`finally` block no matter how the program exits. - Use `subprocess` and `shutil`
to invoke external PDF text tools (for example `pdftotext` from poppler, or
`pdfinfo` for page counts) when they are available on `PATH`; detect
availability with `shutil.which`.
Support at least one backend, and fall back to an alternative or a pure-Python
approach if the preferred tool is missing; if no backend exists, print a helpful
installation hint (for Termux: `pkg install poppler`) and exit. - Use `tempfile`
for any intermediate files, cleaning them up afterwards. - Use
`concurrent.futures.ProcessPoolExecutor` to extract pages in parallel across the
worker count, preserving original page order in the output regardless of
completion order. - Use `textwrap` to wrap long lines to the terminal width
before splitting into screens. - Use `from __future__ import annotations` and
type hints throughout; `typing` helpers such as `Iterable`, `Iterator`,
`Sequence`, and `TYPE_CHECKING` may be used. **Data structures** Use frozen
dataclasses with `slots=True`: - `InputFile`: path (Path), backend (str),
page_count (int). - `ScreenPart`: page_number, part_number, part_count, text.
A page whose wrapped text exceeds the screen height is split into multiple
parts. **Core behavior** 1.
For each input PDF, determine the page count and choose a backend; skip and log
a warning for files that cannot be read or are encrypted without a usable
password. 2.
Extract text per page (in parallel), normalize line endings, strip trailing
whitespace, and collapse excessive blank lines.
Treat pages with no extractable text (e.g., scanned images) by showing a
placeholder message rather than crashing. 3.
Wrap text to the current terminal width (obtained via `shutil.get_terminal_size`
with a fallback of 80×24 when not a TTY).
Split each page into parts that fit the usable height, reserving rows for a
header line and separator. 4.
Build a flat list of screens across all files and pages.
Each screen is rendered by: - Clearing the screen with an ANSI escape sequence
(or `clear`). - Writing a header of the form "Page N | Part X/Y | Screen A/B"
followed by a separator line whose length is the smaller of the header length
and terminal width. - Writing the screen body text followed by a newline, then
flushing stdout. 5.
Interactive navigation via raw key reads: - Space / `f` / Down / `j` / Enter:
next screen. - `b` / Up / `k`: previous screen. - `g` / `G`: first / last
screen. - Number entry for jumping to a page, if feasible. - `q` or Ctrl-C: quit
cleanly with exit status 0 and restore the terminal. - Handle arrow keys
(multi-byte escape sequences) correctly. - Re-render on terminal resize where
practical, by re-checking terminal size before each draw. 6.
Non-interactive mode (when stdout is not a TTY or a flag forces it): write all
screens' text sequentially without clearing the screen or waiting for keys.
**Error handling and edge cases** - Missing files, non-PDF files, unreadable or
corrupt PDFs, password-protected PDFs, and empty documents must each produce a
clear message via loguru without a traceback to the user. - Handle
`KeyboardInterrupt` gracefully everywhere, including during parallel extraction
(shut down the process pool). - Handle broken pipes when stdout is closed early
(e.g., piped to `head`). - If a subprocess call fails or times out, log the
error and treat that page as empty rather than aborting the whole run. - Very
narrow terminals (width under ~20) and very short terminals (height under ~5)
should still function without crashing; clamp minimums. - Very large PDFs: avoid
holding redundant copies of the full text; process page by page where possible.
- Non-UTF-8 extracted bytes should be decoded with `errors="replace"`. - Ensure
the terminal is restored to canonical mode even when an exception occurs.
**Execution details** - The file begins with an executable shebang for the
Termux Python interpreter and includes a `main()` function invoked under `if
__name__ == "__main__":`, which exits with `sys.exit(main())` returning an
integer status code. - Keep the code modular: separate helpers for argument
parsing, backend detection, page extraction, text wrapping and pagination,
screen rendering, key reading, and the navigation loop. - Use `WORKERS` and
`PDF_SUFFIXES` as module-level constants (`PDF_SUFFIXES` a frozenset of
`".pdf"`). - Write concise docstrings and comments only where logic is
non-obvious; match a clean, typed, idiomatic style. **Usage examples to
support** - `pager.py book.pdf` — open an interactive pager for one file. -
`pager.py a.pdf b.pdf --pages 1-10` — several files with a page range (if the
option is implemented). - `pager.py report.pdf | less` — non-interactive output
when piped.
Deliver the complete script as a single file. ---
